## 2. Contract proposal and product behavior

All of this is proposed unless §10 marks it ruled.

### 2.1 Near-misses (NM1, NM3)

**Where.** On every blocker of kind `atom` (positive body atom), at any descent
depth. Not on `negated`, `comparison` or `head` blockers (NM-D6, `(o1)`).

**Normalization `N(v)`** (NM-D4, `(o1)`), defined only for `str`:

1. Unicode NFKC;
2. `str.casefold()`;
3. delete every character whose Unicode general category starts with `P`;
4. replace each maximal run of characters for which `str.isspace()` is true
   with one U+0020, then strip leading and trailing U+0020.

`N(v)` is undefined (no near-match possible) for non-`str` values, and a value
with `N(v) == ""` never near-matches.

**Definition (row-level, NM-D3, `(o1)`).** Let the blocker's atom have roles
`R`, partitioned into constant roles, bound-variable roles `B` and
free-variable roles. For a binding `b` that reached the blocker and a row `f`
of the atom's fact type (the rows the join read, `_Walk.facts`), `(b, f)` is a
**near-miss** when:

- every constant role matches `f` exactly (production identity);
- every role in `B` matches exactly or `N` is defined and equal on both sides;
- at least one role in `B` differs exactly (its `differing` set is non-empty).

Free roles are unconstrained. A blocker with `B` empty has no near-misses (no
value reached it to compare).

**Payload (raw view).**

```json
"near_miss_count": 1,
"near_misses": [
  {"roles": ["party"],
   "sample": {"party": "Acme Corp.", "case": "c-9"},
   "candidate": {"party": "ACME CORP.", "case": "c-9"}}
]
```

`sample` is `b` projected onto `B` (keyed by role, as `samples` is); `candidate`
is `f` projected onto `B`; `roles` is the sorted differing set. Pairs are
distinct by `(sample, candidate)` identity, sorted by canonical JSON of
`[sample, candidate]`, capped at 5 (NM-D5, as WN-D9); `near_miss_count` is the
uncapped number of distinct pairs. Both fields are present on every atom
blocker; an empty list with count 0 means "searched, none".

**Redacted view.** `near_miss_count` is shown; `near_misses` is `"redacted"`.

### 2.2 Disclosure mode (NM2)

**Values (NM-D1 proposal under `(o1)`).**

| Mode | Declared redaction | Witness-lane views (`witness`, `replay`, `why-not`) | Raw requests |
|---|---|---|---|
| unset | today: applied unless `unredacted` | today: API raw, CLI redacted unless `--raw` | today |
| `internal` | not applied (as `unredacted=True`) | raw in API and CLI | allowed (no-ops) |
| `customer` | applied | redacted in API and CLI (`api.why_not` returns the redacted view) | refused: CLI exit 3; API `ValueError` naming the mode |

**Setting it (NM-D2).** Proposed: environment variable `GND_DISCLOSURE` with
values `internal` or `customer`, plus a global CLI option
`gnd --disclosure {internal,customer}` that takes precedence over the variable
for that invocation. No per-call API keyword: the mode belongs to the
deployment, not the caller, and a keyword would let a customer-facing caller
override it. Any other value of the variable is refused at the public boundary
(CLI exit 3, API `ValueError`), never treated as unset.

**What it does not cover.** Exit codes, verdicts, counts and structural fields
are identical across modes; only population-value fields differ. Files GND
writes into a caller-chosen location for later reading by the same caller
(experiment session stores, `--out` certificates) follow the mode at write time;
`customer` refuses raw certificate export. Whether retained session evidence
written before the mode was set must be re-projected is out of scope (§7).

**Reporting.** Outputs that already report a redaction mode keep their field
(`mode: applied | unredacted`); why-not keeps `view: raw | redacted`. No new
field and no report schema bump.

